{"qid":"Q2576305","names":["Duchy of Croatia"],"title":"Duchy of Croatia","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Duchy of Croatia emerged in the mid-eighth century as a Frankish client state carved from the former Roman province of Dalmatia, with its seat at Nin (Nona). Duke Branimir (879–892) obtained papal recognition of Croatian autonomy from Pope John VIII in 879, marking the polity's effective independence. The duchy reached its territorial apex under Tomislav, who united the Dalmatian and Pannonian Croatian lands and received the royal title from Pope John X around 925, transforming the duchy into the Kingdom of Croatia.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The political history is well attested through papal correspondence and Frankish annals, but internal territorial boundaries within the duchy are poorly defined.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Duchy of Croatia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Croatia"},{"label":"Britannica: Croatia — Early history","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Croatia/Early-history"},{"label":"Britannica: Tomislav","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Tomislav"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Duchy of Croatia","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Višeslav","nativeName":"Višeslav","roman":"","start":800,"end":810,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Borna","nativeName":"Borna","roman":"","start":810,"end":821,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Vladislav","nativeName":"Vladislav","roman":"","start":821,"end":835,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":n